[Introduction]
Camera is one of the main object in computer vision. Understanding how a camera
work can actually get us to many interesting problem of computer visions such as
image panorama or 3D reconstruction.

Believe it or not, camera was once invented with motivation from human eyes (ref?)
one of the most complicated organ in a human body (ref?). Despite how complicated
camera technology can get to now, the camera model employed in computer vision
is, surprisingly, kinda simple.

If we put a film in front of an object, we get a very blur image, because light
rays reflected from every part of object will ended at the same position on the
film. Thus, no good

To limit the ray that can touch the film, we put a barrier with small pinhole on
it. That basically makes a camera.

---

Try to not repeat the theory. You are not writing a book. Though, mention and
include import theorical part when necessary. Or make a short summary/story to
incorporate all the necessary/important theory part

The main question I want to target is

1. What is camera parameter intrisic and extrinsic

Intrinsic: shows the internal parameter/characteristic of a camera:
focal length, center of projection, etc.

Extrinsic: shows the relation of camera to a frame called global frame/coordinate
So if we are only interested in image formation given a 3D point in camera coordinate
extrinsic parameter is irrelevant.

2. How are they used?
Converting from pixel coordinate to camera coordinate
Converting from camera coordinate to pixel coordinate
Converting between camera frames

DEMO: python?


Note for later article: Blender is an amazing tool for computer vision. As computer
vision is an inverse problem where we want to recover some unknown information 
from physical world. A forward problem is trying to describe the lighting in
physical world is done by computer graphic. This is how blender is helpful.
Although computer graphic is no way (till now) can be as sophisticated as the 
real physical world, but at some sense it can help us in understanding.

